quently  someone comes in the office they get  this download of data they're  overwhelmed they can't remember it we do  something now called open notes where we  the moment we enter a note in our chart  we sent it to the patient so they have  access to the full medical record note  simultaneous with the rest of the health  care system that they can sit back in  the comfort of their own home and  approaches a person rather than a  patient and the example I like to give  is if you have three options for  treating a 45 year old woman with new  onset of hypertension and diabetes and  one option is maximal benefit maximal  wrist another option is pretty good  benefit but not so much risk a third  option is a pretty good benefit not much  risk and you really don't have to work  that hard to do it and the woman says my  daughter is getting married in three  weeks so give me the easy option for  three weeks and that will cut over to  the high benefit one so I will go ahead  and end on these two slides so what I  believe the evolution we need to seek is  how to use data to create mindfulness  and mindful man and then a quote from  Hubert Humphrey that hangs in health and  human services here in DC that moves me  every time I read it and what he said is  it has been said that the moral test of  government is how that government treats  those who are in the dawn of life the  children those who are in the twilight  of life the elderly and those who are in  the shadows of life the sick the needy  and the handicapped and everything we do  is technologists if we forget the fact  that that the democratization of  technology is a myth it's been exposed  by george packer and numerous writings  we're missing the boat we need to  actively pay attention to how the  benefits of modern technology help  bridge the digital divide and the  growing economic disparities so i don't  know if i have time for any questions  nope all right thank you very much
 

